1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,818
Welcome to episode nine.

2
00:00:01,858 --> 00:00:06,929
Last time we assumed that all the
useful heat went into the water.

3
00:00:06,969 --> 00:00:10,414
In a real calorimeter,
that isn't quite true,

4
00:00:10,454 --> 00:00:15,023
and today we'll fix it with a
technique called calibration.

5
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:16,951
A quick check first.

6
00:00:16,991 --> 00:00:23,158
How much energy warms one hundred and
twenty point zero grams of water by four

7
00:00:23,198 --> 00:00:25,067
point zero zero degrees?

8
00:00:25,107 --> 00:00:26,699
Pause and calculate.

9
00:00:35,133 --> 00:00:41,978
Using q equals m c delta T: one hundred
and twenty point zero times four point one

10
00:00:42,018 --> 00:00:48,863
eight times four point zero zero, which is
two thousand and six point four joules.

11
00:00:48,903 --> 00:00:53,984
Keep that number in mind; it's
close to, but not the same as,

12
00:00:54,024 --> 00:00:56,543
what a real calorimeter needs.

13
00:00:57,533 --> 00:01:01,769
Here's a solution calorimeter:
an insulated cup with a lid,

14
00:01:01,809 --> 00:01:07,824
a measured volume of water, a stirrer, a
temperature probe, and an electric heater.

15
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:12,576
When energy goes in, it
doesn't only warm the water.

16
00:01:12,616 --> 00:01:19,540
It also warms the inside of the cup, the
probe, the stirrer and the heater itself.

17
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:26,119
Those parts are part of what heats up,
so they absorb some of the energy too.

18
00:01:26,466 --> 00:01:30,179
So instead of m c delta
T for the water alone,

19
00:01:30,219 --> 00:01:36,053
we use a single number for the whole
setup: the calibration factor, C F.

20
00:01:36,093 --> 00:01:42,825
It's the energy needed to raise the
temperature of the calorimeter and its contents

21
00:01:42,865 --> 00:01:46,455
by one degree Celsius,
in joules per degree.

22
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:52,188
The calibration factor belongs to this
particular calorimeter with these particular

23
00:01:52,228 --> 00:01:52,776
contents.

24
00:01:52,816 --> 00:01:56,569
Change the cup, the probe, or
the amount of liquid inside,

25
00:01:56,609 --> 00:01:59,160
and the calibration factor changes too.

26
00:02:00,132 --> 00:02:06,202
To find the calibration factor, we put in
a known amount of energy and measure the

27
00:02:06,242 --> 00:02:07,468
temperature rise.

28
00:02:07,508 --> 00:02:13,320
An electric heater is ideal, because we
can measure the energy very precisely.

29
00:02:13,666 --> 00:02:18,263
The electrical energy equals
voltage times current times time:

30
00:02:18,303 --> 00:02:20,282
E equals V times I times t.

31
00:02:20,322 --> 00:02:24,395
Voltage in volts, current in
amps, and time in seconds.

32
00:02:24,435 --> 00:02:28,733
Volts times amps gives watts,
which are joules per second,

33
00:02:28,773 --> 00:02:31,765
so multiplying by seconds leaves joules.

34
00:02:32,132 --> 00:02:33,947
Time must be in seconds.

35
00:02:33,987 --> 00:02:38,738
A four-minute heating period is
two hundred and forty seconds.

36
00:02:38,778 --> 00:02:44,996
Using four instead of two hundred and
forty would make the energy sixty times too

37
00:02:45,036 --> 00:02:45,826
small.

38
00:02:45,866 --> 00:02:52,172
Then the calibration factor is the energy
supplied divided by the temperature rise

39
00:02:52,212 --> 00:02:57,398
it caused: C F equals E over delta
T, in joules per degree Celsius.

40
00:02:58,332 --> 00:03:03,546
We can think of the calibration
factor as two parts stacked together.

41
00:03:03,586 --> 00:03:08,268
The water's part is its mass
times its specific heat capacity.

42
00:03:08,308 --> 00:03:13,523
The apparatus part is the energy
per degree needed by the cup, probe,

43
00:03:13,563 --> 00:03:15,010
stirrer and heater.

44
00:03:15,398 --> 00:03:18,240
Adding them gives the calibration factor.

45
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:21,402
This simple model assumes
the parts just add,

46
00:03:21,442 --> 00:03:27,345
and that their heat capacities don't change
over the small temperature range we use.

47
00:03:27,732 --> 00:03:29,649
Here's a powerful check.

48
00:03:29,689 --> 00:03:35,766
The apparatus always needs some energy
to warm up, so its part is positive.

49
00:03:35,806 --> 00:03:42,453
That means a correctly measured calibration
factor must be larger than the water's

50
00:03:42,493 --> 00:03:46,245
part alone: C F is at
least m c for the water.

51
00:03:46,598 --> 00:03:51,888
If a calculated calibration factor comes
out smaller than the water alone could

52
00:03:51,928 --> 00:03:57,123
account for, something has gone wrong
in the measurements or the calculation.

53
00:03:58,065 --> 00:04:01,811
Practice question Q
seventeen is worth six marks.

54
00:04:01,851 --> 00:04:04,632
Pause the video and work through it.

55
00:04:20,065 --> 00:04:20,547
Part a.

56
00:04:20,587 --> 00:04:26,432
The electrical energy: six point zero
zero volts times one point five zero amps

57
00:04:26,472 --> 00:04:32,467
times two hundred and forty seconds is
two thousand one hundred and sixty joules.

58
00:04:32,507 --> 00:04:36,192
The temperature rose four
point zero zero degrees,

59
00:04:36,232 --> 00:04:42,077
so the calibration factor is two thousand
one hundred and sixty divided by four

60
00:04:42,117 --> 00:04:47,034
point zero zero: five hundred and
forty joules per degree Celsius.

61
00:04:47,398 --> 00:04:53,497
Plausibility check: the water alone needs
one hundred and twenty point zero times

62
00:04:53,537 --> 00:04:59,559
four point one eight, which is five hundred
and one point six joules per degree.

63
00:04:59,599 --> 00:05:04,904
Five hundred and forty is a bit
larger, exactly as the model requires.

64
00:05:05,265 --> 00:05:05,745
Part b.

65
00:05:05,785 --> 00:05:09,087
The apparatus contribution
is the difference:

66
00:05:09,127 --> 00:05:13,542
five hundred and forty minus
five hundred and one point six,

67
00:05:13,582 --> 00:05:17,330
which is thirty-eight point
four joules per degree.

68
00:05:17,370 --> 00:05:19,226
It's small, but not zero.

69
00:05:19,598 --> 00:05:20,059
Part c.

70
00:05:20,099 --> 00:05:24,426
In a later experiment with the
same calorimeter and contents,

71
00:05:24,466 --> 00:05:28,005
the temperature rises five
point six zero degrees.

72
00:05:28,045 --> 00:05:33,445
The heat gained by the calorimeter is
the calibration factor times the rise:

73
00:05:33,485 --> 00:05:36,953
five hundred and forty
times five point six zero,

74
00:05:36,993 --> 00:05:40,317
which is three thousand
and twenty-four joules,

75
00:05:40,357 --> 00:05:43,077
about three point zero two kilojoules.

76
00:05:44,064 --> 00:05:44,617
Part d.

77
00:05:44,657 --> 00:05:50,459
Now the water is increased to one
hundred and fifty point zero grams,

78
00:05:50,499 --> 00:05:53,592
with the same cup, probe and stirrer.

79
00:05:53,632 --> 00:05:57,018
Can we still use five hundred and forty?

80
00:05:57,398 --> 00:05:57,572
No.

81
00:05:57,612 --> 00:06:03,493
The water's part grows: one hundred and
fifty point zero times four point one eight

82
00:06:03,533 --> 00:06:07,846
is six hundred and twenty-seven
point zero joules per degree.

83
00:06:07,886 --> 00:06:11,556
The apparatus part stays at
thirty-eight point four,

84
00:06:11,596 --> 00:06:15,622
assuming the heat capacities
simply add and don't change.

85
00:06:15,662 --> 00:06:20,688
So the new calibration factor is six
hundred and sixty-five point four,

86
00:06:20,728 --> 00:06:24,367
about six hundred and
sixty-five joules per degree.

87
00:06:24,731 --> 00:06:30,138
Marks for question seventeen: two for
the energy and calibration factor,

88
00:06:30,178 --> 00:06:34,601
one for the apparatus contribution,
one for the later heat,

89
00:06:34,641 --> 00:06:37,551
and two for the new calibration factor.

90
00:06:37,591 --> 00:06:42,921
The trap is keeping five hundred and
forty after changing the contents,

91
00:06:42,961 --> 00:06:48,030
or using m c delta T for the water
as if it included the apparatus.

92
00:06:48,398 --> 00:06:54,349
So a calibration factor can only be reused
if the calorimeter and its contents

93
00:06:54,389 --> 00:06:56,039
match the calibration.

94
00:06:56,079 --> 00:07:00,648
In an exam, a question will
either say the setup is matched,

95
00:07:00,688 --> 00:07:04,068
or give you enough
information to adjust it.

96
00:07:05,063 --> 00:07:09,085
Suppose some heat escapes during
the electrical calibration.

97
00:07:09,125 --> 00:07:12,335
Which way does that push
the calibration factor?

98
00:07:12,375 --> 00:07:13,147
Don't guess.

99
00:07:13,187 --> 00:07:14,473
Follow the formula.

100
00:07:14,863 --> 00:07:19,842
The electrical energy is measured
from the voltage, current and time,

101
00:07:19,882 --> 00:07:22,387
so it's correct: it doesn't change.

102
00:07:22,427 --> 00:07:27,406
But some of that energy leaks out
instead of warming the calorimeter,

103
00:07:27,446 --> 00:07:31,955
so the measured temperature rise
is smaller than it should be.

104
00:07:32,330 --> 00:07:35,996
The calibration factor
is E divided by delta T.

105
00:07:36,036 --> 00:07:40,096
The top stays the same, and
the bottom gets smaller,

106
00:07:40,136 --> 00:07:45,104
so the calibration factor comes
out larger than the true value.

107
00:07:45,463 --> 00:07:50,922
This surprises many students, because
they've memorised "heat loss makes the answer

108
00:07:50,962 --> 00:07:51,519
smaller".

109
00:07:51,559 --> 00:07:55,030
That's true for some quantities
and false for others.

110
00:07:55,070 --> 00:08:00,330
The only reliable method is to trace the
affected measurement through the actual

111
00:08:00,370 --> 00:08:01,170
formula.

112
00:08:01,263 --> 00:08:02,055
Checkpoint.

113
00:08:02,095 --> 00:08:08,180
During a calibration, the student records
the heating time as two hundred seconds

114
00:08:08,220 --> 00:08:11,280
when it was really two hundred and forty.

115
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:15,212
Will the calibration factor
be too big or too small?

116
00:08:15,252 --> 00:08:17,294
Pause and trace it through.

117
00:08:25,730 --> 00:08:26,521
Too small.

118
00:08:26,561 --> 00:08:30,927
A shorter recorded time
gives a smaller calculated E.

119
00:08:30,967 --> 00:08:35,787
With the same measured delta
T, E over delta T is smaller.

120
00:08:36,729 --> 00:08:40,367
Practice question Q eighteen
is worth four marks.

121
00:08:40,407 --> 00:08:43,709
Pause the video and
decide what you'd write.

122
00:08:56,129 --> 00:08:58,120
Start with the lower bound.

123
00:08:58,160 --> 00:09:04,364
One hundred point zero grams of water alone
needs one hundred point zero times four

124
00:09:04,404 --> 00:09:08,953
point one eight: four hundred
and eighteen joules per degree.

125
00:09:08,993 --> 00:09:12,564
The cup and probe have a
positive heat capacity,

126
00:09:12,604 --> 00:09:18,206
so the true calibration factor must be
more than four hundred and eighteen.

127
00:09:18,246 --> 00:09:23,512
Three hundred and sixty is less, so
it's inconsistent with this setup.

128
00:09:23,862 --> 00:09:26,033
Now the student's explanation.

129
00:09:26,073 --> 00:09:31,412
Heat loss during calibration makes
the measured temperature rise smaller,

130
00:09:31,452 --> 00:09:34,139
and that makes E over delta T larger.

131
00:09:34,179 --> 00:09:38,560
So heat loss would push the
calibration factor up, not down.

132
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:41,548
It can't explain a value that's too low.

133
00:09:41,929 --> 00:09:43,796
What could make it too low?

134
00:09:43,836 --> 00:09:49,587
Anything that makes the calculated E too
small, or the measured delta T too large.

135
00:09:49,627 --> 00:09:55,450
For example, an overestimated temperature
rise, perhaps from a misread thermometer,

136
00:09:55,490 --> 00:09:59,334
or an underestimated electrical
energy, such as a time,

137
00:09:59,374 --> 00:10:01,917
current or voltage recorded too low.

138
00:10:02,262 --> 00:10:08,148
Marks: one for the lower bound, one for
concluding that three hundred and sixty is

139
00:10:08,188 --> 00:10:13,423
inconsistent, one for the heat-loss
direction, and one for a valid error.

140
00:10:13,463 --> 00:10:19,421
Notice the pattern: a full-credit answer
always says which measurement is affected,

141
00:10:19,461 --> 00:10:24,085
which way it moves, and what that
does to the calculated result.

142
00:10:25,061 --> 00:10:25,747
To recap.

143
00:10:25,787 --> 00:10:32,192
A calorimeter's calibration factor is the
energy needed per degree for the whole

144
00:10:32,232 --> 00:10:34,045
setup and its contents.

145
00:10:34,085 --> 00:10:39,927
Find it electrically, with E equals
V times I times t, with t in seconds,

146
00:10:39,967 --> 00:10:42,425
then C F equals E over delta T.

147
00:10:42,465 --> 00:10:48,306
It must exceed the water's m c, and
it only applies to matching contents.

148
00:10:48,346 --> 00:10:52,858
When something goes wrong,
trace it through the formula.

149
00:10:53,195 --> 00:10:54,291
Closing recall.

150
00:10:54,331 --> 00:11:00,197
A heater runs at twelve point zero volts
and two point zero zero amps for five

151
00:11:00,237 --> 00:11:04,589
minutes, and the temperature
rises six point zero degrees.

152
00:11:04,629 --> 00:11:06,976
What is the calibration factor?

153
00:11:17,395 --> 00:11:20,043
Five minutes is three hundred seconds.

154
00:11:20,083 --> 00:11:25,207
E equals twelve point zero times two
point zero zero times three hundred:

155
00:11:25,247 --> 00:11:27,612
seven thousand two hundred joules.

156
00:11:27,652 --> 00:11:30,017
Divided by six point zero degrees,

157
00:11:30,057 --> 00:11:34,898
the calibration factor is one thousand
two hundred joules per degree.

158
00:11:34,938 --> 00:11:40,557
Next episode: using a calibrated calorimeter
to measure the enthalpy change of a

159
00:11:40,597 --> 00:11:41,397
reaction.
